{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbnbWPH12Vv8jFdqipV6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7417, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7417}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e7fe4b90-6dfd-4924-bd74-c29b2eeb5416", "uuid": "75aa0343-a62b-4c93-a56f-9ddd4ef51850", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:53:33.461Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbnbW3SWjiGsynYG1PSt"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbnbWPH12Vv8jFdqipV6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (round-half-to-even, IEEE 754's default) rounds to the nearest value as usual, but when a number is exactly halfway between two candidates, it rounds to the **nearest even digit**.\n\n| Value | Round half up | Banker's |\n|-------|---------------|----------|\n| 0.5 \u2192 int | 1 | 0 |\n| 1.5 \u2192 int | 2 | 2 |\n| 2.5 \u2192 int | 3 | 2 |\n| 3.5 \u2192 int | 4 | 4 |\n| 2.675 \u2192 2 dp | 2.68 | 2.68 (7 is odd, so up to 8) |\n| 2.665 \u2192 2 dp | 2.67 | 2.66 (6 is even, so stays) |\n\n**Why accountants like it:**\n\n- **No systematic bias.** Round-half-up always pushes ties upward. Over thousands of line items, that drifts the total in one direction. With half-to-even, ties go up about as often as they go down, so errors cancel out.\n- **Totals stay closer to the truth.** The sum of rounded values tracks the sum of the unrounded values better. That matters for tax, interest, allocations, and reconciliations across many transactions.\n- **It's fair to both parties.** Neither the payer nor the payee is consistently favored by the tie-breaking rule.\n- **It's a standard.** IEEE 754 and many financial and regulatory conventions specify it, so results match across systems.\n\n**Caveats:**\n\n- The benefit only shows up when you have many ties. With data that rarely lands exactly on a half, it makes little difference.\n- Binary floats usually can't represent values like 2.675 exactly, so use a decimal type for money. In Python that's `decimal.Decimal` with `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`. Python's built-in `round()` is half-even, but it operates on floats, which gives surprising results.\n- Some jurisdictions or contracts mandate round-half-up, so check the governing rule.\n\nThis project already uses it. Tax amounts use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, a decision made with the finance team in March 2026, so don't switch it to `ROUND_HALF_UP`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7417, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7417}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e7fe4b90-6dfd-4924-bd74-c29b2eeb5416", "uuid": "c93891cf-90b6-464b-b702-6eff7461e4e9", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:53:38.550Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbnbW3SWjiGsynYG1PSt"}
